hermes-office local patch — NOT an upstream pull request.

Target: clawmem@0.43.5, applied against the installed npm package root
(/usr/local/lib/node_modules/clawmem), AFTER 036, 040 and 041.

Why: `reasoning_effort` was validated against one vendor's enum
(none|minimal|low|medium|high|xhigh) and anything else was dropped with a
warning. Command Code's gateway uses a different vocabulary — off|low|medium|
high|xhigh|max — and rejects `none` outright with HTTP 400:

  {"message": "Invalid option: expected one of \"off\"|\"low\"|\"medium\"|
   \"high\"|\"xhigh\"|\"max\"", "type": "invalid_request_error"}

So the two sets do not intersect at the "no reasoning" end. Setting
CLAWMEM_LLM_REASONING_EFFORT=none 400s the request, and setting `off` was
silently ignored — which is worse than an error, because the request then runs
WITH thinking: a reasoning model spends the entire max_tokens budget in
reasoning_content and returns an empty content string. Measured on
deepseek-v4.1-flash with max_tokens=500, the terse QMD expansion prompt:

  reasoning_effort=low   completion 500, reasoning_tokens 500, content ""
  reasoning_effort=off   completion 406, reasoning_tokens 0,   content 406 chars
  reasoning_effort=none  HTTP 400

Fix: widen the accepted set to the UNION of the vocabularies in play and pass
the value through verbatim. `off` is NOT normalized to `none` — that would
reintroduce the 400 it exists to avoid. Normalization still applies to casing
and whitespace, and an unknown value still warns and yields null, so a typo
cannot silently become a different effort.

This is a one-line behavior change with a wide blast radius: the same normalizer
serves the shared CLAWMEM_LLM_* lane, the CLAWMEM_QUERY_* lane and the
CLAWMEM_JUDGE_* lane (all construct through `new LlamaCpp({...})` ->
normalizeRemoteLlmReasoningEffort). Verified: `off` reaches the wire on all
three, `none` still does too (for gateways that accept it), and an empty value
still emits no field at all.

